<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/7th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">Treaty of Hudaybiyyah</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/628">628</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a> · Also known as Treaty of Hudaybiyya</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Treaty of Hudaybiyyah was a truce agreed in March 628 at al-Hudaybiyyah, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a>, between Muhammad and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quraysh">Quraysh</a> of Mecca. It established a ten-year truce and allowed the Muslims to make the pilgrimage the following year. Though it seemed unfavourable to many Muslims, it strengthened Muhammad's position, and after its breakdown he took Mecca in 630.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Muhammad had migrated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a> in 622, and armed conflict followed with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quraysh">Quraysh</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a>, who kept him and his followers from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaaba">Kaaba</a>. In March 628 he set out for the lesser pilgrimage with about 1,500 Muslims, reportedly after a dream in which he circled the Kaaba. The Meccans presumed an attack and sent cavalry to stop them, but Muhammad took an unconventional route and camped at Hudaybiya, on the edge of the sacred territory.</p><p>He told the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quraysh">Quraysh</a> he wished to perform the pilgrimage rather than fight, but they refused to let him enter that year. A rumour that Uthman, sent to negotiate, had been killed led the Muslims to swear the Pledge of the Tree to fight, and when it proved false the Quraysh sent Suhayl ibn Amr to settle terms.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>The treaty set a truce of ten years. A man of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quraysh">Quraysh</a> who fled to Muhammad without his guardian's permission would be returned, while a Muslim who went to the Quraysh would not. Any tribe could enter a covenant with either side.</p><p>The Muslims would go back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a> that year but could make the pilgrimage the next, staying three days while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quraysh">Quraysh</a> left the city, carrying no weapons except sheathed swords. Ali wrote the document. Suhayl objected to the opening Basmala and to the words Messenger of God, and Muhammad accepted the wording Muhammad, son of Abdullah to preserve the agreement; according to tradition, he erased the phrase himself when Ali was reluctant to.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Some followers, Umar among them, reportedly objected to the terms. Abu Jandal, Suhayl's son, converted and was returned to his father under the return clause, but Muhammad did not return believing women who emigrated, citing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quran">Quran</a> 60:10. Abu Basir, who was also returned, escaped and with other converts raided Meccan caravans until the Meccans asked Muhammad to take them in.</p><p>The treaty held for about two years, until the Banu Bakr, allies of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quraysh">Quraysh</a>, attacked the Banu Khuza'ah, allies of Muhammad, with help from some Quraysh. The Quraysh would not meet Muhammad's demands, and in 630 he took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a>. Historians regard the treaty as an important stage in his consolidation of power, since the Quraysh implicitly treated him as an equal.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Treaty of al-Hudaybiya” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Treaty of Hudaybiyya</b> in 628 brought a truce.</blockquote><a href="/state/islamic-state-of-medina">Islamic state of Medina</a></li></ul></section>
